<!-- BOOK STRUCTURE -->
<section id="services" class="section-alt-2">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header" data-scroll-reveal>
      <span class="eyebrow">Seven Parts</span>
      <h2>The Book's Architecture</h2>
      <p class="lead">A systematic journey through the foundations, systems, and practice of Hindu philosophy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">I</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Vedic &amp; Upanishadic Foundations</h3>
          <p>The source texts and philosophical origins that underpin all subsequent Hindu thought. Understanding the Vedas, Upanishads, and the primordial worldview.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">II</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Metaphysical Concepts</h3>
          <p>Core philosophical ideas about being, consciousness, and reality. Exploring Brahman, Atman, Maya, and the nature of existence itself.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">III</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Cosmological &amp; Ethical Concepts</h3>
          <p>Understanding the structure of the universe, cosmic cycles, dharma, karma, and the ethical dimensions of human existence.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">IV</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>The Six Classical Darshanas</h3>
          <p>Samkhya, Yoga, Mimamsa, Vedanta, Nyaya, and Vaisheshika — the orthodox schools of Hindu philosophy and their distinct worldviews.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">V</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Four Vedanta Schools</h3>
          <p>Advaita, Vishishtadvaita, Dvaita, and Shuddhadvaita. Non-dualism, qualified non-dualism, dualism, and the Pushtimarg tradition.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">VI</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Nastika Systems</h3>
          <p>Carvaka materialism, Jainism, and Buddhism — the heterodox schools that challenge Vedic authority while remaining part of Hindu philosophical dialogue.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-scroll-reveal>
        <div class="card-icon">VII</div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Sadhana as Lived Practice</h3>
          <p>Philosophy in action. How spiritual discipline, meditation, and devotion transform understanding into liberation and self-realization.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="card-link">Explore <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

